# =====================================================================================================
# GENERATED LOCALIZATION ARTIFACTS ARE CHECKED OUT EXACTLY AS THE GENERATOR WRITES THEM (TEST-002).
# =====================================================================================================
#
# `SSAS.Localization.CatalogTool` emits both files with a hard-coded CRLF — `GeneratedCatalogWriter` holds
# `private const string NewLine = "\r\n"` and the client serializer explicitly rewrites every `\n` to it.
# There is no `Environment.NewLine` anywhere in the tool, deliberately: the generated bytes are meant to be
# identical on every platform.
#
# `verify` then compares the regenerated bytes to the file on disk with an exact `SequenceEqual`. Without a
# rule here the repository had no line-ending policy, so Git materialised these LF-in-index files as CRLF on
# Windows and LF on Linux. The comparison therefore passed on one operating system and could not pass on the
# other — and the artifacts were correct in both cases.
#
# Pinning the CHECKOUT to CRLF matches the generator instead of changing it. The alternative — moving the
# generator to LF — would have meant rewriting committed generated output to fix a checkout concern.
#
# ---- DELIBERATELY TWO EXACT PATHS, NOT A PATTERN.
#
# `*.cs eol=crlf` or `*.json eol=crlf` would impose a line-ending policy on the whole repository to satisfy
# two generated files. These are the only files whose bytes are compared against a generator's output, so
# they are the only files that need the rule.
src/BuildingBlocks/SSAS.BuildingBlocks.Localization/Generated/LocalizationCatalog.Generated.cs text eol=crlf
src/BuildingBlocks/SSAS.BuildingBlocks.Localization/Generated/localization-catalog.client.generated.json text eol=crlf
